================================================================================
1) WHAT IS QUORUM? (MOST IMPORTANT)
================================================================================

We define:

N = total replicas
W = replicas needed for write success
R = replicas needed for read success

Example:
N = 3

Possible:
W = 2
R = 2

Meaning:
- write successful after 2 ACKs
- read from any 2 replicas

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY THIS WORKS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Because:
write quorum and read quorum overlap.

Golden rule:
R + W > N

This overlap means:
at least one read replica likely saw latest write.

================================================================================
2) STEP-BY-STEP WORKING WITH EXAMPLE
================================================================================

Suppose:

N = 3
Nodes = A, B, C
W = 2
R = 2

Initial:
price = 100

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WRITE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Client updates:
100 → 120

Sent to:
A, B, C

A ACK ✅
B ACK ✅
C timeout ❌

Write SUCCESS because W=2 reached.

Now state:
A = 120
B = 120
C = 100

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
READ
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Client reads from:
B and C

Gets:
120 and 100

Since one node overlaps with write quorum,
latest can be detected.

This is quorum overlap magic.

================================================================================
3) DOES QUORUM GIVE STRONG CONSISTENCY?
================================================================================

This is the deep Jordan confusion point.
Let’s break it honestly.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SHORT ANSWER
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

NOT ALWAYS.

Quorum gives:
"probabilistic strong-ish consistency"

NOT true linearizable strong consistency by default.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY PEOPLE THINK IT IS STRONG
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Because:
R + W > N

So overlap guaranteed.

Looks strong.

BUT real distributed systems have edge cases.

================================================================================
4) WHY QUORUM IS NOT TRULY STRONGLY CONSISTENT
================================================================================

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CASE 1: CONCURRENT WRITES
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Two clients write same key simultaneously.

Write1:
A,B = 10

Write2:
B,C = 20

Both satisfy W=2.

Now read from:
A,C

Result:
10 and 20

No single truth.

This is NOT strong consistency.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CASE 2: CLOCK / VERSION RESOLUTION
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Even with overlap,
if versions resolved incorrectly
(stale timestamp / wrong vector merge),
stale reads possible.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CASE 3: READ FROM STALE COORDINATOR
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Coordinator may choose stale node first,
especially in async replication.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CASE 4: PARTITION + FAILED NODE RECOVERY
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Node comes back after long downtime.
Old data may still satisfy read quorum temporarily.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
INTERVIEW GOLD LINE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Quorums improve consistency guarantees, but without synchronous
replication + strict version ordering they are not fully linearizable.

================================================================================
5) REAL DEEP INTUITION — WHY NOT STRONG?
================================================================================

Strong consistency means:
once write SUCCESS returns,
NO future read should ever see old value.

Leaderless quorum cannot guarantee this in all failure cases because:
- writes can race
- network delays happen
- replicas may ACK different versions
- repair is asynchronous

So:
eventual consistency + quorum overlap
≠ strict strong consistency

This is a FAANG favorite trap.

