6) FAILED WRITES (VERY IMPORTANT)
================================================================================

Now the next big Jordan topic.

Example:
N = 3
W = 2

Client writes:
status = shipped

A ACK ✅
B timeout ❌
C timeout ❌

Only 1 ACK.

Write FAILED returned.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
BIG QUESTION
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Did write actually happen?

Possible cases:

1) only A stored it
2) B stored but ACK lost
3) C stored late
4) all eventually stored after timeout

So client sees:
FAILED

But database may contain:
PARTIAL WRITE

This is dangerous.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
REAL WORLD EXAMPLE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Payment marked failed on client,
but one replica actually processed it.

Later stale read confusion.

================================================================================
7) WHY FAILED WRITES ARE DANGEROUS
================================================================================

Problems:
- retries can duplicate writes
- idempotency required
- client uncertain state
- partial visibility
- reconciliation complexity

Best practice:
use write IDs / request IDs.

================================================================================
8) SLOPPY QUORUMS — WHY THEY EXIST
================================================================================

Normal quorum assumes:
write to HOME replicas only.

But what if home replica nodes are down?

Should system reject write?

High availability systems say:
NO.

Instead use:
SLOPPY QUORUM

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
INTUITION
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Write to temporary healthy nodes.

Later move data back.

This keeps system available during failures.

================================================================================
9) SLOPPY QUORUM EXAMPLE
================================================================================

Home replicas for key:
A, B, C

B and C down ❌

Normally:
W=2 impossible

Without sloppy quorum:
WRITE FAILS

With sloppy quorum:
Write to:
A + D + E

Where:
D,E are fallback nodes

Write succeeds.

This is:
best effort quorum over reachable nodes.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
REAL LIFE ANALOGY
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Customer package ka original warehouse बंद hai.
Nearby temporary warehouse pe parcel store kar diya.
Later correct warehouse me move.

================================================================================
10) DOES SLOPPY QUORUM PRESERVE STRONG GUARANTEE?
================================================================================

NO.
This makes consistency weaker.

Why?

Because read may still go to:
A,B,C

But write stored at:
A,D,E

So read quorum may MISS latest write.

This is huge.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
IMPORTANT CONCLUSION
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Sloppy quorum improves availability
at cost of quorum overlap guarantees.

This is why:
R + W > N rule may effectively break.

================================================================================
11) HINTED HANDOFF CONNECTION
================================================================================

Sloppy quorum usually paired with:
HINTED HANDOFF

Temporary node D stores:
"this belongs to B"

When B comes back:
D forwards missed write.

Then temporary copy removed.

This restores eventual correctness.

================================================================================
12) TRADEOFFS (INTERVIEW GOLD)
================================================================================

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NORMAL QUORUM
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

+ better consistency
+ quorum overlap
+ simpler reasoning
- lower availability if home replicas fail

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SLOPPY QUORUM
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

+ very high availability
+ survives replica outage
+ great for writes during partitions
- weaker read freshness
- overlap guarantee breaks
- hinted handoff complexity

================================================================================
13) WHEN TO USE WHAT
================================================================================

NORMAL QUORUM:
- product catalog
- user profile
- order status
- moderate consistency needed

SLOPPY QUORUM:
- shopping cart
- social likes
- metrics
- logs
- high availability systems

Avoid sloppy quorum for:
- money
- transactions
- strict stock count

================================================================================
14) FINAL INTERVIEW ANSWER STRUCTURE
================================================================================

If asked:
"Do quorums guarantee strong consistency?"

Perfect answer:

1) quorum overlap improves freshness
2) R + W > N ensures intersection
3) but concurrent writes break linearizability
4) failed writes create uncertain partial state
5) sloppy quorum weakens overlap further
6) hinted handoff restores eventual correctness
7) therefore quorum is not strict strong consistency

This answer is senior-level.

================================================================================
15) ONE-LINE MEMORY HOOKS
================================================================================

- Quorum = enough replicas
- R + W > N = overlap
- Overlap != strict strong consistency
- Failed write may still partially commit
- Sloppy quorum = temporary fallback replicas
- Availability ↑ consistency guarantees ↓
- hinted handoff restores home replicas

